Model-based reinforcement learning (RL) has recently gained significant attention due to its capacity to learn from limited data and its ability to generalize to unseen environments. However, these approaches often rely on complex, opaque models that are difficult to interpret. This paper introduces a novel hierarchical RL framework that utilizes inductive logic programming (ILP) to generate interpretable, symbolic representations of the environment. By employing ILP to learn a symbolic model of the dynamics, the system can be applied to various RL tasks, including those involving unseen objects and environments. We demonstrate the method's effectiveness through experiments on a robotic arm tasked with stacking blocks. The results indicate that the proposed approach successfully learns a symbolic model of the dynamics and generalizes effectively to unseen objects and environments. Furthermore, we show that the learned symbolic model can be used to predict the outcomes of actions. Finally, we highlight the method's ability to transfer knowledge across different tasks. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/robin-lee/ILP-RL.

Keywords: model-based reinforcement learning, symbolic reinforcement learning, inductive logic programming, hierarchical reinforcement learning, interpretability, knowledge transfer.

