Constrained reinforcement learning (CRL) seeks to maximize expected rewards while adhering to additional constraints. This paper introduces a novel inverse CRL framework designed to learn a reward function from expert demonstrations. We propose a new algorithm that utilizes a constrained maximum entropy approach to infer the reward function, ensuring the resulting policy aligns with the provided expert trajectories. We provide theoretical analysis of the algorithm's sample complexity and present empirical results demonstrating its effectiveness. Furthermore, we show that this method can be applied to recover reward functions for both discrete and continuous control tasks. Finally, we illustrate the utility of our approach in the context of imitation learning. The source code is accessible at https://github.com/ChenYi2019/InverseCRL.

Keywords: Inverse Reinforcement Learning, Constrained Reinforcement Learning, Maximum Entropy.
